# Commit 009 — Enterprise Domain Integration & Boundary Map

## Purpose

The Enterprise Domain Integration & Boundary Map establishes how the business domains defined within EDA-001 relate to one another without creating unnecessary coupling.

The objective is to ensure that Essentials Mart can evolve from:

* 1 store → 10,000+ stores;
* 100 users → 100 million+ users;
* 1 country → global deployment;

without requiring the enterprise architecture to be fundamentally redesigned.

This section establishes:

* domain ownership;
* data ownership;
* domain responsibilities;
* bounded-context boundaries;
* cross-domain communication;
* integration contracts;
* domain events;
* integration events;
* governed data consumption;
* AI Society boundaries;
* Trust Engine boundaries;
* Learning Engine boundaries;
* failure isolation;
* and architectural review requirements.

---

# 1. Enterprise Boundary Principle

Each major business domain must have a clearly defined responsibility and an authoritative source of truth.

Domains may collaborate extensively.

They must not become dependent upon one another's internal implementation.

The following principle therefore applies:

> **A domain may depend on another domain's capability or published contract, but it must not depend on that domain's internal implementation.**

This allows individual domains to evolve, scale, or eventually be separated into independent services without forcing changes throughout the enterprise.

---

# 2. Bounded Context Principle

Each major business domain represents a bounded architectural context.

A bounded context defines the boundary within which a particular business model, terminology, rules and data ownership apply.

Different domains may therefore represent the same real-world concept differently when their business responsibilities require it.

Essentials Mart will not attempt to force every domain into one universal internal model.

This prevents the enterprise from becoming one tightly coupled model.

---

