Together they establish a much stronger enterprise foundation.

---

# 90. Relationship to EDA-001 Part 3

Commit 016 establishes the **resilience requirements**.

EDA-001 Part 3 will later define the deeper **security architecture**, including:

* cyber resilience;
* secure recovery;
* backup protection;
* immutable evidence;
* key management;
* infrastructure security;
* network resilience;
* incident response;
* disaster recovery security;
* identity recovery;
* compromise containment.

The security architecture should therefore build on the resilience model rather than duplicate it.

---

# 91. Final Architectural Principle

> **Essentials Mart must not merely work when everything works. It must remain trustworthy when things fail.**

A resilient Essentials Mart should be capable of:

```text
Detecting Failure
       ↓
Containing Failure
       ↓
Protecting Critical Operations
       ↓
Degrading Gracefully
       ↓
Preserving Data
       ↓
Recovering Services
       ↓
Reconciling State
       ↓
Verifying Integrity
       ↓
Recording What Happened
       ↓
Learning From It
```

That is the standard required for a platform intended to grow from a single store into a globally deployable enterprise system.

# EDA-001 — ESSENTIALS MART ENTERPRISE ARCHITECTURE

## PART 2 — ENTERPRISE OPERATING MODEL, COORDINATION & DECISION ARCHITECTURE

**Commit:** 018
**Status:** COMMIT READY
**Position:** Final substantive commit of EDA-001 Part 2
**Predecessor:** Commit 017 — Enterprise Scalability & Global Growth Architecture

---

# 1. Purpose

Commit 018 establishes how the Essentials Mart enterprise operates as a coordinated system.

Previous commits established the enterprise domains, intelligence capabilities, actors, relationships, events, analytics, trust mechanisms, resilience and scalability.

Those capabilities must now be connected by an explicit operating model.

The purpose of this commit is therefore to establish:

* how domains cooperate;
* how responsibilities are distributed;
* how decisions are made;
* how decisions move between humans, systems and AI;
* how cross-domain processes operate;
* how conflicts are resolved;
* how exceptions are handled;
* how enterprise policies interact with domain autonomy;
* how accountability is preserved;
* how the model scales without creating a central bottleneck.

This commit does **not** define the final database schema, APIs, service topology or implementation details.

Those belong to subsequent architectural phases.

---

# 2. Core Principle

> **Every significant enterprise capability must have an owner, every significant decision must have an authority, every significant process must have an accountable outcome, and every cross-domain interaction must have an explicit coordination mechanism.**

No architectural capability should exist in an ownership vacuum.

---

# 3. The Enterprise Operating Model

Essentials Mart shall operate as a **federated, intelligence-enabled enterprise**.

This means:

* enterprise-wide concerns are governed centrally where consistency is essential;
* domain-specific decisions remain with the domain where local context matters;
* cross-domain decisions use explicit coordination mechanisms;
* AI may automate appropriate decisions within defined authority;
* humans retain accountability for decisions that require human ownership;
* enterprise policy overrides conflicting local preference where enterprise-wide consistency is required.

The objective is neither complete centralisation nor unrestricted decentralisation.

It is:

> **Centralise what must be consistent. Federate what benefits from domain ownership. Coordinate what crosses boundaries.**

This principle prevents the enterprise from becoming either a central bottleneck or a collection of disconnected systems.

---

# 4. Enterprise Operating Layers

The operating model consists conceptually of five layers:

```text
Enterprise Strategy
        ↓
Enterprise Policies & Guardrails
        ↓
Domain Capabilities
        ↓
Cross-Domain Coordination
        ↓
Operational Execution
```

Intelligence operates across these layers rather than existing as an isolated layer.

---

# 5. Enterprise Strategy

Enterprise strategy establishes:

* long-term direction;
* enterprise objectives;
* acceptable risk;
* business priorities;
* customer promise;
* economic objectives;
* geographic expansion;
* major investment priorities.

Domains must align with enterprise strategy.

A domain must not optimise itself in a way that materially damages the enterprise.

---

# 6. Enterprise Guardrails

Enterprise guardrails establish boundaries within which domains operate.

Examples include:

* security requirements;
* privacy requirements;
* financial constraints;
* ethical requirements;
* AI governance;
* regulatory requirements;
* trust requirements;
* customer protection;
* data governance.

Guardrails establish boundaries without necessarily prescribing every implementation detail.

---

# 7. Domain Autonomy

Within enterprise guardrails, domains should retain appropriate autonomy.

For example:

```text
Enterprise
   ↓
Defines Customer Protection Principles

Customer Intelligence
   ↓
Determines appropriate customer insights
```

The enterprise should not micromanage every domain decision.

---

# 8. Domain Ownership

Every domain shall have a clearly defined conceptual owner.

The owner is accountable for:

* domain purpose;
* domain outcomes;
* domain policies;
* domain data;
* domain intelligence;
* domain health;
* domain dependencies;
* domain evolution.

Exact organizational role assignments will be defined later.

---

# 9. Domain Boundaries

A domain owns its responsibilities.

It must not silently assume ownership of another domain's responsibilities.

For example:

```text
Reward Intelligence
        ≠
Trust Engine
```

Reward Intelligence may consume trust information.

It does not own the Trust Engine.

Likewise:

```text
Customer Intelligence
        ≠
Customer Identity
```

Customer Intelligence may analyse customer information.

It does not automatically own identity.

---

# 10. Cross-Domain Coordination

When a process crosses multiple domains, coordination must be explicit.

Conceptually:

```text
Domain A
   ↓
Event / Request
   ↓
Domain B
   ↓
Decision
   ↓
Domain C
   ↓
Outcome
```

No domain should need undocumented assumptions about another domain's internal behaviour.

---

# 11. Enterprise Value Streams

The enterprise shall recognise end-to-end value streams.

Examples include:

### Customer value stream

```text
Discover
 ↓
Consider
 ↓
Purchase
 ↓
Fulfil
 ↓
Receive
 ↓
Use
 ↓
Feedback
 ↓
Relationship
 ↓
Re-engagement
```

### Supplier value stream

```text
Supplier Onboarding
 ↓
Catalogue
 ↓
Supply
 ↓
Inventory
 ↓
Sales
 ↓
Performance
 ↓
Returns / Complaints
 ↓
Settlement
```

### Service-resolution value stream

```text
Issue
 ↓
Detection
 ↓
Service Desk
 ↓
Investigation
 ↓
Resolution
 ↓
Customer Outcome
 ↓
Learning
```

The detailed process models will be produced later.

Part 2 establishes that the enterprise must think in terms of **end-to-end outcomes**, not isolated departmental actions.

---

# 12. Process Ownership

Every major enterprise process must eventually have an accountable owner.

A process owner is responsible for the outcome across domain boundaries.

This prevents the situation where:

```text
Domain A says:
"Not our responsibility."

Domain B says:
"Not our responsibility."

Customer receives:
"No resolution."
```

Cross-domain processes must therefore have outcome accountability.

---

# 13. Decision Rights

The enterprise shall explicitly distinguish:

* who decides;
* who executes;
* who must be consulted;
* who must be informed;
* who is accountable.

Decision rights must be defined at the appropriate level.

The goal is to avoid both:

* uncontrolled decentralisation;
* unnecessary centralisation.

Clear decision rights are particularly important for AI-enabled operations, where ownership must remain explicit even when execution becomes automated.

---

# 14. Decision Hierarchy

Decisions should generally occur at the lowest appropriate level.

Conceptually:

```text
Enterprise Decision
        ↓
Domain Decision
        ↓
Process Decision
        ↓
Operational Decision
        ↓
Automated Decision
```

A lower-level actor should not escalate every ordinary decision to enterprise leadership.

---

# 15. Enterprise-Level Decisions

Enterprise-level authority applies to matters such as:

* enterprise strategy;
* enterprise-wide policy;
* major risk;
* global standards;
* major financial exposure;
* security architecture;
* major AI authority;
* major regulatory obligations.

---

# 16. Domain-Level Decisions

Domains should decide matters that primarily concern their own capability.

Examples:

* domain workflows;
* domain-specific intelligence;
* domain-specific optimisation;
* domain-specific operational rules.

Subject to enterprise guardrails.

---

# 17. Operational Decisions

Operational decisions should occur as close as practical to the operation.

Examples:

* stock handling;
* service actions;
* routine fulfilment;
* routine customer assistance;
* store operations.

This supports responsiveness and scalability.

---

# 18. AI Decision Rights

AI agents may receive decision authority only when explicitly granted.

AI authority must be:

* scoped;
* identifiable;
* policy-bound;
* auditable;
* revocable;
* appropriate to the risk of the action.

AI must not inherit unlimited authority simply because it can technically access a system.

---

# 19. Human Decision Rights

Humans retain authority over decisions designated as requiring human judgement.

These may include:

* high-risk decisions;
* irreversible decisions;
* exceptional cases;
* policy changes;
* major financial decisions;
* sensitive customer cases;
* security decisions;
* enterprise strategic decisions.

---

# 20. Automated Decision Rights

Low-risk, well-defined decisions may be automated.

Automation is encouraged where it improves:

* speed;
* consistency;
* scalability;
* cost;
* customer experience.

But automation must remain within explicit authority.

---

# 21. Decision Risk Classification

Future architecture shall classify decisions according to risk.

Conceptually:

```text
Level 1 — Routine
Level 2 — Material
Level 3 — High Risk
Level 4 — Critical
```

The higher the risk, the stronger the requirements for:

* authorization;
* evidence;
* oversight;
* audit;
* reversibility;
* escalation.

---

# 22. Decision Reversibility

Decision authority should consider whether an action can be reversed.

A reversible action may require less authority than an irreversible action of similar operational scope.

Examples:

```text
Recommendation
→ Easily reversible

Temporary account preference
→ Reversible

Financial transaction
→ Material

Permanent data deletion
→ Potentially irreversible
```

---

# 23. Escalation

When an actor cannot safely or legitimately make a decision, the decision must escalate.

Conceptually:

```text
Local Decision
      ↓
Unable to Decide
      ↓
Domain Authority
      ↓
Unable to Decide
      ↓
Enterprise Authority
```

Escalation must not become the default path for ordinary operations.

---

# 24. Exception Management

Exceptions are expected in a complex enterprise.

The operating model shall support:

* identification;
* classification;
* ownership;
* temporary mitigation;
* decision;
* approval;
* documentation;
* learning;
* closure.

An exception must not silently become permanent architecture.

---

# 25. Policy Hierarchy

Policies shall have an explicit hierarchy.

Conceptually:

```text
Law / Regulation
      ↓
Enterprise Policy
      ↓
Domain Policy
      ↓
Process Rule
      ↓
Operational Rule
      ↓
Automation
```

Lower-level rules must not contradict higher-level constraints.

---

# 26. Policy Precedence

Where policies conflict:

> **The higher-authority policy takes precedence unless an explicitly approved exception exists.**

This principle prevents domain-level optimisation from accidentally violating enterprise obligations.

---

# 27. Domain Coordination Rules

Domains must coordinate when:

* one domain depends on another;
* a process crosses ownership boundaries;
* data is exchanged;
* a decision affects another domain;
* an event changes another domain's state;
* an enterprise objective requires multiple domains.

---

# 28. Event-Based Coordination

Events shall remain a primary coordination mechanism where appropriate.

A domain should communicate meaningful state changes without exposing unnecessary internal implementation details.

Conceptually:

```text
Domain State Change
       ↓
Enterprise Event
       ↓
Interested Domains
```

This supports loose coupling.

---

# 29. Request-Based Coordination

Not every interaction is an event.

Some operations require direct requests.

The architecture therefore recognises both:

```text
Request/Response
```

and:

```text
Event-Driven Coordination
```

The final API and integration strategy will be defined later.

---

# 30. Intelligence Coordination

Intelligence engines must cooperate without collapsing their boundaries.

For example:

```text
Customer Intelligence
       ↓
Customer Context

Trust Engine
       ↓
Trust Context

Reward Intelligence
       ↓
Reward Decision

Relationship Intelligence
       ↓
Relationship Context
```

The enterprise can combine these signals without turning every engine into one monolithic intelligence system.

---

# 31. Intelligence Composition

Higher-level decisions may combine outputs from multiple intelligence engines.

Conceptually:

```text
Customer Intelligence
        +
Trust Intelligence
        +
Relationship Intelligence
        +
Transaction Context
        ↓
Enterprise Decision Context
```

The detailed orchestration mechanism belongs to the EIP and AI architecture.

---

# 32. Trust in Cross-Domain Decisions

A domain must not blindly trust information simply because it came from another internal domain.

Cross-domain information must have:

* identifiable provenance;
* appropriate ownership;
* defined semantics;
* appropriate authorization.

---

# 33. Data Ownership

Every important enterprise information asset must eventually have an owner.

The owner is accountable for:

* meaning;
* quality;
* lifecycle;
* authorized use;
* changes;
* stewardship.

The precise data ownership model will be established during Phase 2.

---

# 34. Analytics Ownership

Analytics consumers must not automatically become owners of the source information.

For example:

```text
Supplier Analytics
```

may use:

```text
Sales Information
```

without owning the underlying transaction information.

This distinction prevents analytical duplication from creating conflicting sources of truth.

---

# 35. Systems of Record

Phase 2 must establish authoritative sources for important enterprise information.

The enterprise should avoid multiple competing sources of truth.

Where multiple representations are necessary, lineage and ownership must remain clear.

---

# 36. Information Flow

Information should move according to purpose and authority.

The operating model shall distinguish:

```text
Source
 ↓
Owner
 ↓
Authorized Consumer
 ↓
Transformation
 ↓
Decision
```

This becomes a foundation for the Information Architecture phase.

---

# 37. Lifecycle Governance

Important enterprise objects must eventually have defined lifecycle states.

Examples include:

```text
Created
 ↓
Active
 ↓
Suspended
 ↓
Modified
 ↓
Archived
 ↓
Retired
```

The exact lifecycle for each entity will be defined in Phase 2.

Part 2 establishes that lifecycle ownership is mandatory.

---

# 38. Accountability

Every major enterprise outcome must have an accountable party.

Accountability cannot be assigned solely to:

* "the system";
* "the AI";
* "the platform";
* "the algorithm".

A system may execute.

An AI may recommend.

A service may automate.

But accountable ownership must remain identifiable.

---

# 39. AI Accountability

AI agents must have:

* an owner;
* an authority boundary;
* a defined purpose;
* defined capabilities;
* monitoring;
* auditability;
* escalation paths.

The more consequential the agent's actions, the stronger these requirements become.

---

# 40. AI Escalation

AI agents should be capable of recognising when they cannot safely continue.

Conceptually:

```text
AI Decision
   ↓
Within Authority?
   ↓ YES
Execute

   ↓ NO
Escalate
```

AI should not be rewarded architecturally for bypassing its authority boundary.

---

# 41. Human-AI Collaboration

The operating model shall support:

```text
Human → AI
AI → Human
AI → AI
System → AI
AI → System
```

but every interaction must remain governed by identity, authority and accountability.

---

# 42. AI Society Coordination

The AI Society shall not be a collection of autonomous agents acting without coordination.

Agents must operate as participants in an enterprise operating model.

This means:

* defined responsibilities;
* defined capabilities;
* defined authority;
* defined escalation;
* defined communication mechanisms;
* defined accountability.

---

# 43. No Agent Sovereignty

No AI agent is sovereign over the enterprise.

An agent cannot independently redefine:

* enterprise policy;
* security policy;
* its own authority;
* another agent's authority;
* enterprise ownership;
* its own accountability.

---

# 44. Human Override

Where appropriate, authorized humans must be able to:

* pause;
* override;
* revoke;
* disable;
* review

AI actions.

The exact mechanism belongs to later architecture.

---

# 45. Conflict Resolution

Cross-domain conflicts must have a defined resolution path.

Conceptually:

```text
Domain A
    ↕
Domain B
    ↓
Unable to Resolve
    ↓
Enterprise Arbitration
```

The enterprise should resolve conflicts according to:

1. legal requirements;
2. enterprise policy;
3. customer protection;
4. security;
5. strategic objectives;
6. measurable business impact.

---

# 46. Customer Outcome Priority

Where appropriate, cross-domain decisions should consider the effect on the customer.

No domain should optimise a local KPI at the expense of the overall customer relationship.

For example:

```text
Short-Term Margin
        ≠
Always Best Customer Outcome
```

Enterprise-level optimisation should consider long-term value.

---

# 47. Supplier Coordination

Supplier-facing processes must coordinate:

* supplier management;
* catalogue;
* inventory;
* sales;
* complaints;
* returns;
* logistics;
* settlement;
* performance intelligence.

No individual supplier domain should be treated as isolated from the wider value chain.

---

# 48. Store Coordination

Stores represent physical operational nodes within the enterprise.

Store operations must coordinate with:

* inventory;
* customer experience;
* fulfilment;
* staff;
* suppliers;
* Walk Mode;
* analytics;
* service operations.

---

# 49. Walk Mode Coordination

Walk Mode must not operate as an isolated digital experience.

It must coordinate with:

```text
Store
Inventory
Products
Customer
Navigation
AI Companion
Analytics
Enterprise Events
```

This preserves the principle that Walk Mode represents the **Living Digital Supermarket** rather than a separate virtual product.

---

# 50. Reward Coordination

Reward Intelligence must coordinate with:

* customer behaviour;
* transaction information;
* Trust Engine;
* relationship intelligence;
* financial constraints;
* enterprise strategy.

This prevents rewards from being optimised independently of business economics or trust.

---

# 51. Service Coordination

Customer service functions such as:

* service desk;
* returns;
* information point;
* parcel collection

must ultimately connect into real operational ownership.

The system must not create the illusion of support without an accountable human or operational process behind it.

---

# 52. Enterprise Analytics Coordination

Analytics must aggregate information across domains without destroying domain ownership.

The enterprise should be able to produce:

```text
Customer View
Supplier View
Store View
Staff View
Management View
Enterprise View
```

while preserving access boundaries.

---

# 53. Role-Specific Operating Views

Different actors require different operational views.

Examples:

### Customer

* personal spending;
* favourite products;
* purchase history;
* rewards;
* relevant recommendations.

### Supplier

* product performance;
* sales;
* returns;
* complaints;
* breakages;
* supply performance.

### Staff

* operational workload;
* authorized customer/service information;
* role-specific analytics;
* activity records.

### Management

* enterprise performance;
* operational health;
* financial indicators;
* strategic analytics.

The architecture therefore treats **role-specific information views as an enterprise operating-model requirement**, not merely a UI feature.

---

# 54. Auditability

Every significant operational action must eventually be attributable to:

* a human;
* service;
* device;
* AI agent;
* automated process.

The operating model therefore depends on the audit architecture established earlier.

---

# 55. Operational Learning

Enterprise operations should feed learning systems.

Conceptually:

```text
Operation
 ↓
Outcome
 ↓
Measurement
 ↓
Learning
 ↓
Improvement
 ↓
New Operation
```

This creates an enterprise learning loop.

---

# 56. Closed-Loop Enterprise Intelligence

Essentials Mart should eventually operate as a feedback system:

```text
Observe
 ↓
Understand
 ↓
Decide
 ↓
Act
 ↓
Measure
 ↓
Learn
 ↓
Improve
```

This is one of the defining architectural characteristics of the platform.

---

# 57. Preventing Local Optimisation

A domain must not optimise a local metric if doing so materially harms an enterprise outcome.

For example:

```text
Domain KPI
      ↓
Enterprise Impact
      ↓
Decision
```

Enterprise-level intelligence should be capable of detecting such conflicts.

---

# 58. Enterprise KPI Hierarchy

The architecture should eventually distinguish:

* enterprise KPIs;
* domain KPIs;
* process KPIs;
* operational KPIs;
* customer KPIs;
* AI performance KPIs;
* security KPIs.

The detailed metrics catalogue belongs later.

---

# 59. Governance by Exception

Governance should focus attention where risk or impact warrants it.

Routine operations should remain automated or domain-managed.

Exceptional situations should receive greater oversight.

This prevents governance from becoming a bottleneck.

---

# 60. Change Management

Enterprise changes must consider cross-domain impact.

A change to one domain may affect:

* events;
* APIs;
* data;
* analytics;
* intelligence engines;
* AI agents;
* security;
* Walk Mode;
* suppliers;
* stores.

Therefore architectural change must eventually include impact analysis.

---

# 61. Dependency Awareness

Domains must declare meaningful dependencies.

Dependencies should eventually be represented explicitly in the enterprise model.

This enables:

* impact analysis;
* failure analysis;
* change planning;
* capacity planning;
* security analysis.

---

# 62. Enterprise Coordination at Scale

At 100 million users, manual coordination becomes impossible.

Therefore coordination must increasingly rely on:

* events;
* policies;
* automation;
* intelligence;
* explicit ownership;
* standardized interfaces;
* observability.

Human coordination should focus on exceptional and strategic situations.

---

# 63. Local Autonomy at Scale

Global scale requires local adaptation.

Stores and regional operations may require local decisions based on:

* geography;
* demand;
* supply;
* infrastructure;
* regulations;
* customer behaviour.

Local autonomy must operate within enterprise guardrails.

---

# 64. Global Consistency

Certain capabilities must remain consistent globally where required.

Examples include:

* identity principles;
* security principles;
* core customer protection;
* auditability;
* enterprise event semantics;
* AI governance;
* fundamental trust principles.

---

# 65. Localisation Without Architectural Fragmentation

Regional differences must not create completely separate architectures.

The enterprise should support:

```text
Global Core
+
Regional Adaptation
```

rather than:

```text
Country A Architecture
Country B Architecture
Country C Architecture
...
```

---

# 66. Emerging-Market Operation

The operating model must remain viable under:

* intermittent connectivity;
* constrained infrastructure;
* local payment differences;
* regional supplier structures;
* varying logistics capabilities.

This is an operating-model requirement, not merely an infrastructure problem.

---

# 67. Enterprise Memory

The enterprise must retain institutional knowledge.

Important decisions, incidents, changes and lessons should eventually become part of enterprise knowledge.

This supports:

* AI Society;
* human operators;
* architecture;
* training;
* operational learning.

---

# 68. Architectural Memory

Architecture itself must be traceable.

Future ADRs will record why major architectural decisions were made.

This commit establishes that the operating model must preserve the relationship between:

```text
Decision
 ↓
Reason
 ↓
Authority
 ↓
Implementation
 ↓
Outcome
```

---

# 69. Decision Evidence

Important decisions should eventually have sufficient evidence to explain:

* why the decision occurred;
* who/what made it;
* what information was considered;
* which policy applied;
* what outcome resulted.

This is particularly important for AI-driven decisions.

---

# 70. Reversibility and Recovery

Where possible, enterprise decisions should be designed for recovery.

A system that can make a wrong decision but cannot recover from it is less resilient than one that can detect and reverse mistakes.

---

# 71. Enterprise Operating Constitution

This commit establishes the conceptual foundation for an **Essentials Mart Operating Constitution**.

The Operating Constitution is the collection of principles governing:

* authority;
* accountability;
* ownership;
* coordination;
* decision rights;
* policy hierarchy;
* escalation;
* exceptions;
* AI authority;
* human authority;
* domain autonomy.

It is not a legal constitution.

It is an architectural operating contract.

---

